{
  "requirement": "For a Cargo workspace that depends on `habeas`, interactive `cargo agents sync` MUST let the user enable Habeas's plugin. Once enabled, it MUST make the skills shipped with the selected Habeas version available to configured agents. After the selected version changes, `cargo agents sync` MUST provide the skills shipped with the new version.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural obligation in `requirements.integration.symposium` is that Habeas must declare a Symposium plugin, so `cargo agents sync` can offer it to the user and, once it is enabled, supply the skills shipped with the selected version. The clauses on behavior (interactive enablement, providing the skills again after a version change) come from Symposium reading the crate metadata. The Habeas source does not implement them.\n\nThree roots in `Cargo.toml` implement the declaration:\n- `table[package.metadata.symposium]/pair[name]` (`name = \"habeas\"`) declares the plugin. Without it Symposium has no Habeas plugin to enable.\n- `table[package.metadata.symposium.defaults]/pair[skills]` (`skills = false`) makes the skills opt-in. That fits the requirement that the user can enable the plugin and that skills are provided once it is enabled.\n- `table[package.metadata.symposium.skills]/pair[source.path]` (`source.path = \"assets/skills\"`) points at the skills shipped inside the crate. `assets/skills/**/SKILL.md` exists in the repository. The only `package.exclude` entry is `evidence/**`, so the skills ship with the crate. Because the metadata is read from the selected Habeas version's own manifest, the skills follow that version. This is why a version change supplies the new version's skills.\n\nThe parent tables carry no further obligation beyond these pairs. The `symposium.installations` and `symposium.hooks` entries belong to `requirements.integration.symposium_cli` and `requirements.integration.symposium_setup_hook`. They are outside this requirement.\n\nbehavioral: The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `requirements.integration.symposium`.\n\n1. **Interactive `cargo agents sync` lets the user enable the plugin.**\n   - `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py#def[main]` calls `interactive_sync`. That function runs `cargo-agents sync` under a pty and waits for the \"Enable it?\" prompt. It answers the prompt and fails if no prompt appeared or the exit code was non-zero.\n   - `main` then asserts that the Symposium config records `auto-enable` for habeas. It also asserts that the hook registration exists. This shows the plugin was enabled through the interactive path.\n\n2. **Once enabled, the skills shipped with the selected version are available to configured agents.**\n   - The consumer config sets up a codex agent.\n   - `main` asserts that every skill directory in `assets/skills` with a `SKILL.md` exists under `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and has byte-identical contents. It also asserts that at least one skill ships.\n   - The marked test `tests/repository.rs#fn[published_package_contains_the_bundled_skills_and_ci_example]` checks that the published package lists every skill named by the `package.metadata.symposium` skills path, plus the CI reference. Together these show the skills reach consumers.\n\n3. **After the selected version changes, `cargo agents sync` provides the new skills.**\n   - `main` waits out Symposium's debounce, then builds a next-version crate. The crate has a bumped version and a modified `SKILL.md`.\n   - It points the consumer at that crate and checks through `cargo metadata` that the next version is selected. It runs `symposium sync` and asserts that the installed skill now equals the changed content.\n   - A sync that kept the old skill would fail this assertion.\n\nI found no untested obligation. Only the first skill's content is changed in the version-change step. The requirement does not distinguish between skills, so I do not count that as a gap.",
  "references": "b6515685b4a0e07b534212abedc2a6b155afa5eb3dd6a4aad0840592a7756628",
